                "speakerRef": "spk:0",
                "tStart": 2511,
                "tEnd": 2525,
                "text": "Product and fashion. Thank you so much, and that brings me to my next question to Miss Ritu. Uh Miss Ritu, if you're there on this, I wanted to understand which industry have you seen a rapid growth where creativity has played a vital role?"
              },
              "textSha256": "a94e33648ab6bce0b5d9b4064783e7bb370fb41692dca1d716cce3978a8bddec"
            }
          ]
        },
        "originalMigration": {
          "available": true,
          "reason": null,
          "artifact": {
            "available": true,
            "path": "data/toc-migrated/2026-08-24-uniaccess-leeds-arts-university/turns.json",
            "revision": "907c9c9434f474622181bbc179f6ac90d75853b9",
            "sha256": "fd835b78b01a869731e56e8de8775f0104f505f5f82e2272be9e8499917d60b8",
            "bytes": 76742
          },
          "matches": [
            {
              "record": {
                "_id": "2026-08-24-uniaccess-leeds-arts-university-t040",
                "tenantId": "toc",
                "sessionId": "2026-08-24-uniaccess-leeds-arts-university",
                "speakerRef": "unknown",
                "tStart": 3208,
                "tEnd": 3508,
                "text": "Juben Thakur: Absolutely. I think I think I very beautifully uh can sum up this that it's just not the brand, it's just the fit, you know, where where where student would want to be excelling and learning and being happy and translating the skills into into where the industry is leading right now. And that brings me to one of the beautiful questions. But I would like to share the screen since you highlighted about, you know, it's not about the university, it's more about the fitment. Let me quickly talk about some things in Leeds, which is very interesting. If you see my screen, there are a lot of Yorkshire Sculpture Park, and as I reiterated earlier as well, Leeds is a very beautiful and artistic city. It's very vibrant. It's very easy on students. It's very diverse. You know, it's it's very interesting if somebody wants to find artists and passion in them. This is one of the Kirkgate Market, which is wide known as the Leeds City Market. If if people know, Marks & Spencer was born in England, was born in Leeds, and that originated right at the bottom where you see the green gates. This is where Marks & Spencer as a brand was born in. And this is the legacy, Leeds City Market, where international students are exempted to go ahead and take the privilege of working 20 hours a week. And that's where a student usually goes because there are a lot of brands, there are a lot of shops inside where student can look for odd jobs in terms of hospitality. This is one of the Leeds Dock in the evening, which is right in the center of the city, where students do go ahead to unwind themselves in the evening. Talking about, you know, one of the most important things in design industry is networking. And that's exactly what we are doing right now to understand universities much better on one-on-one rather than these presentations to involve in conversations. I'm very proud to say that during my visit to Leeds as well, even I was clueless, but then I have started using this in my slides. We have something called as Leeds Digital Festival. I would give you a small homework as well before you go today. Please go ahead and type Leeds Digital Festival, or if you can go to Leeds Arts University campus tour, and if you can have a look at this 1-minute video about how our animation students has worked with graphics students and lit the entire city that came together to see the light show. There are very beautiful light shows that our students at Leeds Arts University do. Also not to forget that there are a lot of opera houses, one of the biggest opera houses, and every month, there's some sort of film festival happening at Leeds. So every student has the opportunity. I I would always say this to my student that not every time you'll get access to Aishwarya Rai and Shah Rukh Khans out there when they're there. But at least as a student, you can always walk up to the team, put in your resume, start networking, understand how this can really shape up your career. And also, we have one of the biggest festivals which is going on, which is called Leeds Concert. I think this is the industry that we're talking about, which is not depreciating. But every time there is always an increment not just to technology, but also UK as a as a wider community. I know there has been times where, you know, everybody has questions that, \"Oh, there are no jobs in UK. You know, UK has gone down. UK has been like this, like that.\" But trust me, I was there at the last intake of this universities, and I am still there and I'm doing my second intake. The need and the want has not changed, although the circumstances of the countries have changed because of the policies, but the demand has never changed. And every country is still trying, especially UK as a design sector, is pumping nearly £104 to £160 billion in the creative careers. And that's why Leeds is not just becoming one of the most emerging universities, but the only specialist universities for those students. I'm I'm extremely proud to say this, that I know it could be a little biased, but I have to say this. But when I was there at the university for a span of week, I met 35 course leaders. I also met four higher education directors, the pro-vice-chancellor, the vice-chancellor of the university. And I I was surprised that I met a cohort of Indian students of first year, second year, third year, and final year students as well, and every faculty that I met, they knew my students' name. And it is as much as surprising to you as it was for me when I was there. But yes, we do know the name of each Indian students in the classroom, and we will talk more about this at the Q&A, because we do not have interviews just like other universities. We have uh our offers are made on the quality, not on the quantity of applications, and not just on the portfolios again. The course structure is very, very, very intensive. It's it's it's the orientation week that goes into the industry experts coming in and interacting with you, then you get familiarized with the technology. Then every year, there is end of the year show. The end of the year show for this year was called Big Bash Bosh. And also there are creative practices that we do. It's just not about the best portfolios to apply to the best schools. It's more about the process that needs in the design sector. Of all the people who have logged in right now, I can see that you do a lot of portfolio making, and everybody asks me, \"Juben, what is a good portfolio?\" I always tell my student, it's not about a good portfolio, but it's mere existence of your ideas into those beautiful structures. So I would like to see the versatility in your thought process from an idea to execution. I think that makes a portfolio and an interviewer to understand that you're open for possibilities. This is a screen for most of the educators out here, which you will realize the entry requirements is not that difficult. Because the university's existence is more about nurturing the creative brains. For a foundation students, it's minimum of 55%. For an undergraduate course is 65%. For most of you all dealing with the PG, it's nearly 55% in any graduation with the IELTS. Scholarship: we have a range of scholarships, specifically for Indian students. A minimum of £18,000—a minimum of £18,000 per year is the fee for undergraduate and postgraduate course. And also we offer a minimum of £2,000 scholarship. It could go more depending on the availability and the quality of the portfolio. This is something that I'm going to show you a little quick, because I really want to get into a little bit of question and answer, because dealing with the design industry is quite tricky, like Bhakti mentioned earlier, you know. So I would like to emphasize and throw some light on that as well. Some of the campuses, which you see state-of-the-art facility, helping students in public university to get printing and stationery for free. We also invest a lot of time getting students equipped to metal and wood workshops. Trust me, in India, it's considered to be a very, very non-organic growth, but that's not what international curriculum are designed for. We feel just because you're learning fashion doesn't mean you can get into fashion jobs. There are a lot of jobs in the metal industry that you can use your fashion tactics or use metal workshops in your industry with wood as well. Music studios, music performance, textile, auditorium, library. And before I jump to this beautiful exciting part that I show to my people, I also wanted to go ahead and wanted to understand from let me Miss Poonam. Miss Poonam, are you there in this session, if I may ask you?"
              },
